The attached image (Figure 5 from the PDF) illustrates how Nano-11 nanoparticles enhance antigen transport and cellular uptake in draining lymph nodes. Panel A's flow cytometry plots show that Nano-11 increases the mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) of ovalbumin (OVA) in OVA-positive cells and the percentage of OVA-containing myeloid cells compared to OVA alone or with aluminum hydroxide (AH). Panel B quantifies this, showing Nano-11 leads to significantly higher OVA MFI per cell, while Panel C demonstrates that Nano-11 specifically targets OVA to migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs).

The image significantly contributes to vaccine development by demonstrating Nano-11's ability to efficiently deliver antigens to crucial antigen-presenting cells, especially migratory dendritic cells, in draining lymph nodes. This enhanced antigen targeting and uptake per cell suggests that Nano-11 can improve the potency of subunit vaccines, potentially allowing for lower antigen doses while still inducing robust immune responses, thereby optimizing vaccine efficacy and cost-effectiveness.